res.setContentType("application/pdf");
res.setHeader("Content-Disposition", "attachment; filename=\"invoice.pdf\"");
res.setContentLengthLong(Files.size(path));
try (OutputStream out = res.getOutputStream()) { Files.copy(path, out); }
It uses getOutputStream because the data is binary, and a stream copy so that the whole file isn't held in memory. The file's path must not come straight from user input. Content-Disposition: inline shows it in the browser instead.

| getWriter() | getOutputStream()
Type | PrintWriter (characters) | ServletOutputStream (bytes)
Use for | HTML, JSON, text | PDF, images, ZIP, file downloads
Encoding | Uses the response charset; set it before calling getWriter() | None, you write raw bytes
Mixing both | Calling one after the other on the same response throws IllegalStateException
res.setContentType("application/pdf");
res.setHeader("Content-Disposition", "attachment; filename=\"report.pdf\"");
try (OutputStream out = res.getOutputStream()) {
    out.write(pdfBytes);
}
Rule of thumb: text goes through the writer, binary goes through the stream, and you pick one per response.
